<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot name="amr_ros_dg" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- ============================== multi-robot fleet support ==============================
       robot_name: settable per-instantiation (fleet.launch.py passes robot_name:=robot1,
       robot2, ... when this xacro is run once per fleet member). Used ONLY to prefix
       gz-sim sensor <topic> overrides below (camera/d435i, camera/floor, ultrasonic_N/scan,
       lidar scan) - those are gz-transport GLOBAL topic strings, so two robots both
       publishing a literal "camera/d435i" topic would collide/merge into one stream.

       Link/joint names ("base_link", "left_wheel", etc.) are deliberately left UNPREFIXED,
       even though this xacro is instantiated once per robot in one shared Gazebo world.
       This was a genuine design decision, not an oversight:
         - gz-sim scopes model/link/sensor ENTITIES per spawned model instance (each
           `ros_gz_sim create -name robotN` call in fleet.launch.py creates its own model
           tree) - link names only need to be unique WITHIN a model, not world-wide, so
           "base_link" repeated across N spawned models does not collide in gz-sim itself.
         - robot_state_publisher mirrors URDF link/joint names verbatim into published TF
           frame_ids, with no automatic prefixing. Running it namespaced (robotN/) makes it
           publish to the RELATIVE topic "tf" -> "/robotN/tf", which is what actually keeps
           each robot's TF tree isolated (per-namespace /tf topic), NOT the frame_id text.
           Keeping frame_ids as plain "base_link"/"odom"/"map" is exactly what Nav2 and
           SLAM Toolbox expect by default inside a ROS namespace - this is the standard,
           documented multi-robot Nav2 pattern (see the turtlebot3 multi-robot demo), and
           is the choice fleet.launch.py's TF design comment builds on (see item 2 there).
       So the only thing that genuinely needs robot_name string-prefixing is gz-transport
       topic names, handled below at each sensor's <topic> element. -->
  <xacro:arg name="robot_name" default="amr_ros_dg" />
  <xacro:property name="robot_name" value="$(arg robot_name)" />

  <!-- namespace_controller_manager: fleet.launch.py sets this true (see its xacro Command
       substitution) so the gz_ros2_control plugin below puts each robot's controller_manager
       under /robotN/, matching robot_state_publisher's namespaced /robotN/robot_description
       topic. Left false by default (gazebo.launch.py, single-robot, does not set it) so the
       existing single-robot pipeline keeps its unnamespaced /controller_manager, /joint_states,
       /diff_drive_controller/cmd_vel topics unchanged - wasd_teleop.py, web_dashboard.py, and
       the RViz config all assume those are unnamespaced today. -->
  <xacro:arg name="namespace_controller_manager" default="false" />
  <xacro:property name="namespace_controller_manager" value="$(arg namespace_controller_manager)" />

  <!-- enable_cameras: default true (single-robot pipeline unchanged). fleet.launch.py can
       pass this false to skip the d435i/floor camera <sensor> elements entirely - GPU-
       rendered RGB cameras (30Hz/15Hz) were a real, measured contributor to Gazebo Sim's
       GUI render cost on WSL2 (confirmed live: gz sim server+gui alone used ~300% CPU with
       3 robots x 2 cameras each). The camera LINKS/meshes stay either way (cheap, no
       rendering cost) - only the actual <sensor> (and its GPU image generation) is skipped. -->
  <xacro:arg name="enable_cameras" default="true" />
  <xacro:property name="enable_cameras" value="$(arg enable_cameras)" />

  <!--
    Migrated from the SolidWorks SW2URDF catkin export (see legacy/ros1_original_package/
    amr_ros_dg_original.urdf for the raw ROS 1 output this was derived from).
    All mass / center-of-mass / inertia / joint-origin values are taken verbatim from the
    exporter CSV (already in meters / kilograms) — nothing here is invented.

    The current meshes/*.STL (from the "AMR-ROS" re-export) are individually isolated, in
    meters, and centered on each link's own coordinate system. No mesh scale factor is
    needed. One CSV glitch in that re-export (caster_b_l mass/inertia came back as all
    zeros) is not used here; caster_b_l below keeps its valid values from the original good
    export. Old backups of broken mesh sets are kept under legacy/ for reference.

    Kinematic tree:
      base_link
        * left_wheel_joint (continuous, axis 0 1 0)  to left_wheel
        * right_wheel_joint (continuous, axis 0 1 0) to right_wheel
        * caster_f_l_joint (continuous, axis 0 0 1)   to caster_f_l
            * caster_wheel_f_l_joint (continuous)     to caster_wheel_f_l
        * caster_f_r_joint to caster_f_r to caster_wheel_f_r_joint to caster_wheel_f_r
        * caster_b_r_joint to caster_b_r to caster_wheel_b_r_joint to caster_wheel_b_r
        * caster_b_l_joint to caster_b_l to caster_wheel_b_l_joint to caster_wheel_b_l

    GAZEBO PHYSICS NOTES (Gazebo Sim / dartsim, no suspension modeled):
      - Drive wheels provide traction (mu=1.0); casters are tip-over support only, not a
        third traction point - their collision sits ~0.5mm above the drive wheel contact
        plane on purpose, so they only touch when the chassis pitches/rolls enough to need
        it, the same way a shopping cart's casters barely load until you lean it.
      - All continuous joints need a non-zero <limit effort>. dartsim treats effort=0 (the
        original SW2URDF placeholder for "passive, no motor") as fully locked, not free.
      - Both drive wheels use the same axis direction (0 1 0). diff_drive_controller sends
        identical-sign velocity commands to both wheel joints assuming positive = forward on
        each; mirroring the axis for the mirrored mesh (as the raw export did) desyncs that.
      - Collision cylinder radii for the wheels are trimmed by fractions of a millimeter from
        their true CAD-measured values (commented at each site below). A rigid contact solver
        with no suspension is sensitive even to ~0.01mm cross-wheel height differences -
        confirmed by joint_states matching left/right drive wheel velocity to 10 decimal
        places while the chassis still visibly arced, so the asymmetry was in which side had
        more ground support, not in the commanded velocity.
  -->

  <xacro:property name="mesh_scale" value="1 1 1" />
  <!-- Industrial color scheme: dark orange chassis, dark grey caster housings, dark blue
       wheels (drive + caster) - was previously one pale near-white color across the robot. -->
  <xacro:property name="chassis_color" value="0.75 0.48 0.05 1" />
  <xacro:property name="caster_housing_color" value="0.15 0.16 0.18 1" />
  <xacro:property name="wheel_color" value="0.07 0.11 0.22 1" />

  <material name="amr_chassis_orange">
    <color rgba="${chassis_color}" />
  </material>
  <material name="amr_caster_grey">
    <color rgba="${caster_housing_color}" />
  </material>
  <material name="amr_wheel_darkblue">
    <color rgba="${wheel_color}" />
  </material>

  <!-- ============================== base_link ============================== -->
  <link name="base_link">
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="-0.0310943353661127 0.00027531991751642 -0.12487536514683" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <mass value="21.3046134591674" />
      <inertia
        ixx="0.29613665577097" ixy="0.00195367305083866" ixz="4.4307398501902E-05"
        iyy="0.422473357982575" iyz="-2.20145006753063E-07" izz="0.697298060229463" />
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://amr_ros_dg/meshes/base_link.STL" scale="${mesh_scale}" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="amr_chassis_orange" />
    </visual>
    <!-- No collision: wheels/casters alone provide ground contact. A chassis collision box
         would overlap the running gear asymmetrically and tilt the robot in dartsim. -->
  </link>

  <!-- ============================== drive wheel macro ============================== -->
  <xacro:macro name="drive_wheel"
    params="name mesh
            mass cx cy cz ixx ixy ixz iyy iyz izz
            jx jy jz ax ay az
            col_radius col_length col_oy">
    <link name="${name}">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="${cx} ${cy} ${cz}" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <mass value="${mass}" />
        <inertia ixx="${ixx}" ixy="${ixy}" ixz="${ixz}" iyy="${iyy}" iyz="${iyz}" izz="${izz}" />
      </inertial>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <geometry>
          <mesh filename="package://amr_ros_dg/meshes/${mesh}" scale="${mesh_scale}" />
        </geometry>
        <material name="amr_wheel_darkblue" />
      </visual>
      <!-- Collision is a cylinder, not the STL mesh (dartsim can't build mesh collision).
           Rotated 90deg about X so the cylinder's Z-axis lines up with the wheel's real
           rolling axis (Y). radius/length/y-offset measured from the wheel STL's bounding
           box; col_radius is nudged a fraction of a mm from that measured value - see the
           GAZEBO PHYSICS NOTES above and the comment at the two xacro:drive_wheel calls. -->
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0 ${col_oy} 0" rpy="1.5707963 0 0" />
        <geometry>
          <cylinder radius="${col_radius}" length="${col_length}" />
        </geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>
    <joint name="${name}_joint" type="continuous">
      <origin xyz="${jx} ${jy} ${jz}" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <parent link="base_link" />
      <child link="${name}" />
      <axis xyz="${ax} ${ay} ${az}" />
      <!-- velocity raised 30.0 -> 60.0 to match the <ros2_control> command_interface limits
           below (see the "Raised from 30.0 -> 60.0" comment there) - dartsim enforces this
           joint-level limit independently of the command_interface's own min/max. -->
      <limit effort="20.0" velocity="60.0" />
      <dynamics damping="0.1" friction="0.2" />
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- col_radius on both wheels: the real jz values put the right wheel's bottom 0.06mm
       lower than the left's (genuine CAD/export tolerance). Radii nudged by ~0.03mm each way
       (not jz, not the visual mesh) so both wheel bottoms sit on the exact same plane. -->
  <xacro:drive_wheel name="left_wheel" mesh="left_wheel.STL"
    mass="0.585665991952606"
    cx="-0.000108392816681013" cy="-0.0253615400637083" cz="-0.000239450250214657"
    ixx="0.000928521729613518" ixy="-1.14250082228267E-10" ixz="1.00749289393112E-09"
    iyy="0.00168853495795271" iyz="-2.52341752508342E-10" izz="0.000928564707830947"
    jx="0.00010849" jy="0.2895" jz="-0.16929" ax="0" ay="1" az="0"
    col_radius="0.07503" col_length="0.051" col_oy="-0.025" />

  <xacro:drive_wheel name="right_wheel" mesh="right_wheel.STL"
    mass="0.585665991952566"
    cx="0.000186897669876618" cy="0.0253615400637088" cz="-0.000184809864538615"
    ixx="0.000928527905827836" ixy="-1.96980205741672E-10" ixz="-1.51100007177081E-08"
    iyy="0.00168853495795271" iyz="1.94752032949863E-10" izz="0.000928558531615229"
    jx="-0.00018706" jy="-0.2895" jz="-0.16935" ax="0" ay="1" az="0"
    col_radius="0.07497" col_length="0.051" col_oy="0.025" />

  <gazebo reference="left_wheel">
    <mu1>1.0</mu1>
    <mu2>1.0</mu2>
  </gazebo>
  <gazebo reference="right_wheel">
    <mu1>1.0</mu1>
    <mu2>1.0</mu2>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- ============================== caster corner macro ============================== -->
  <!-- Each caster corner = swivel joint (continuous, axis 0 0 1) -> housing link
       -> rolling joint (continuous, axis ~= local X) -> wheel link.
       Caster role: tip-over/pitch support only, not a traction point - see w_col_radius
       comment below the macro for how that's enforced in the collision geometry. -->
  <xacro:macro name="caster_corner"
    params="name wheel_name housing_mesh wheel_mesh
            sx sy sz
            h_mass h_cx h_cy h_cz h_ixx h_ixy h_ixz h_iyy h_iyz h_izz
            wx wy wz wax way waz
            w_mass w_cx w_cy w_cz w_ixx w_ixy w_ixz w_iyy w_iyz w_izz
            h_col_sx h_col_sy h_col_sz h_col_ox h_col_oy h_col_oz
            w_col_radius w_col_length">
    <link name="${name}">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="${h_cx} ${h_cy} ${h_cz}" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <mass value="${h_mass}" />
        <inertia ixx="${h_ixx}" ixy="${h_ixy}" ixz="${h_ixz}" iyy="${h_iyy}" iyz="${h_iyz}" izz="${h_izz}" />
      </inertial>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <geometry>
          <mesh filename="package://amr_ros_dg/meshes/${housing_mesh}" scale="${mesh_scale}" />
        </geometry>
        <material name="amr_caster_grey" />
      </visual>
      <!-- No housing collision: it would add friction torque on the swivel joint and
           prevent free rotation. Only the caster wheel (below) provides ground contact. -->
    </link>
    <joint name="${name}_joint" type="continuous">
      <origin xyz="${sx} ${sy} ${sz}" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <parent link="base_link" />
      <child link="${name}" />
      <axis xyz="0 0 1" />
      <!-- effort=2.0 (not 0, not a large number): non-zero avoids dartsim treating this
           passive joint as locked; kept small (this ~30g housing needs no more) so a bad
           contact-solve step can't inject a large spurious torque that never damps out.
           damping=0.02 (not 0): lets the caster settle into its trailing orientation instead
           of oscillating on every start from rest. Never driven through ros2_control. -->
      <limit effort="2.0" velocity="30.0" />
      <dynamics damping="0.02" friction="0.0" />
    </joint>

    <link name="${wheel_name}">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="${w_cx} ${w_cy} ${w_cz}" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <mass value="${w_mass}" />
        <inertia ixx="${w_ixx}" ixy="${w_ixy}" ixz="${w_ixz}" iyy="${w_iyy}" iyz="${w_iyz}" izz="${w_izz}" />
      </inertial>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
        <geometry>
          <mesh filename="package://amr_ros_dg/meshes/${wheel_mesh}" scale="${mesh_scale}" />
        </geometry>
        <material name="amr_wheel_darkblue" />
      </visual>
      <!-- Collision is a cylinder, not the STL mesh. Rotated 90deg about Y so the cylinder's
           Z-axis lines up with the caster wheel's real rolling axis (~X). -->
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 1.5707963 0" />
        <geometry>
          <cylinder radius="${w_col_radius}" length="${w_col_length}" />
        </geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>
    <joint name="${wheel_name}_joint" type="continuous">
      <origin xyz="${wx} ${wy} ${wz}" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <parent link="${name}" />
      <child link="${wheel_name}" />
      <axis xyz="${wax} ${way} ${waz}" />
      <!-- Same reasoning as the swivel joint above: effort=2.0 (non-zero, but small - this
           121g wheel needs no more), damping=0.01 (non-zero, so residual spin bleeds off). -->
      <limit effort="2.0" velocity="30.0" />
      <dynamics damping="0.01" friction="0.0" />
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- w_col_radius on all 4 corners: this puts the caster wheel bottom ~0.5mm ABOVE the
       drive wheel bottom (target contact plane z=-0.24432, same for both wheel types), not
       level with it. The casters are a tip-over/pitch backstop, not a permanent contact
       point - the drive wheels alone should always be the ones bearing weight and traction,
       and a caster only engages once the chassis pitches or rolls enough to close that
       0.5mm gap, exactly like a shopping cart's casters barely touching until you lean it.
       Same radius (0.03229) on all 4 corners even though the real CAD radii differ slightly
       corner to corner (0.0376 vs 0.0377) - see the cross-wheel-height sensitivity note in
       the file header. -->

  <!-- front-left -->
  <xacro:caster_corner name="caster_f_l" wheel_name="caster_wheel_f_l"
    housing_mesh="caster_f_l.STL" wheel_mesh="caster_wheel_f_l.STL"
    sx="0.275" sy="0.17" sz="-0.15478"
    h_mass="0.0343773761631997"
    h_cx="-0.000211217029093325" h_cy="-0.00488343337310898" h_cz="-0.0278047373445326"
    h_ixx="7.07816892636274E-06" h_ixy="1.60117057840084E-07" h_ixz="-1.13833743869238E-08"
    h_iyy="1.07770901763347E-05" h_iyz="-2.74463122286201E-07" h_izz="1.35458216911075E-05"
    wx="0.00064749" wy="0.014986" wz="-0.05225" wax="1" way="0" waz="0"
    w_mass="0.121190564506901"
    w_cx="0.000260006277754798" w_cy="-1.12338994395222E-05" w_cz="-5.55111512312578E-17"
    w_ixx="8.85486982719961E-05" w_ixy="-1.52127688306059E-06" w_ixz="-1.20806832920016E-19"
    w_iyy="5.34047883182945E-05" w_iyz="1.85942777553269E-19" w_izz="5.33390596302369E-05"
    h_col_sx="0.0525" h_col_sy="0.0689" h_col_sz="0.0645"
    h_col_ox="0" h_col_oy="-0.00655" h_col_oz="-0.032"
    w_col_radius="0.03229" w_col_length="0.0365" />

  <!-- front-right -->
  <xacro:caster_corner name="caster_f_r" wheel_name="caster_wheel_f_r"
    housing_mesh="caster_f_r.STL" wheel_mesh="caster_wheel_f_r.STL"
    sx="0.275" sy="-0.17" sz="-0.15478"
    h_mass="0.034377376177769"
    h_cx="-2.21908344033572E-07" h_cy="-0.00488799899349841" h_cz="-0.0278047373438943"
    h_ixx="7.07125079899759E-06" h_ixy="1.59066340376663E-12" h_ixz="4.74709220715998E-10"
    h_iyy="1.07840083029693E-05" h_iyz="-2.74698674033133E-07" h_izz="1.35458216896774E-05"
    wx="0" wy="0.015" wz="-0.05225" wax="1" way="0" waz="0"
    w_mass="0.121190564506901"
    w_cx="0.000260248852002487" w_cy="-2.08166817117217E-17" w_cz="-5.55111512312578E-17"
    w_ixx="8.86144269600542E-05" w_ixy="1.25769959385598E-19" w_ixz="-1.11966583470812E-19"
    w_iyy="5.33390596302359E-05" w_iyz="-3.58784234590011E-19" w_izz="5.33390596302365E-05"
    h_col_sx="0.0514" h_col_sy="0.0670" h_col_sz="0.0645"
    h_col_ox="0" h_col_oy="-0.0065" h_col_oz="-0.032"
    w_col_radius="0.03229" w_col_length="0.0350" />

  <!-- back-right -->
  <xacro:caster_corner name="caster_b_r" wheel_name="caster_wheel_b_r"
    housing_mesh="caster_b_r.STL" wheel_mesh="caster_wheel_b_r.STL"
    sx="-0.275" sy="-0.17" sz="-0.15478"
    h_mass="0.0343773762451283"
    h_cx="-2.21909206808701E-07" h_cy="-0.00488799904923643" h_cz="-0.0278047373832745"
    h_ixx="7.07125079999909E-06" h_ixy="1.59079082659352E-12" h_ixz="4.74709238887088E-10"
    h_iyy="1.07840083034564E-05" h_iyz="-2.74698674016186E-07" h_izz="1.35458216910767E-05"
    wx="0" wy="0.015" wz="-0.05225" wax="1" way="0" waz="0"
    w_mass="0.121190564506901"
    w_cx="0.000260248852002418" w_cy="-2.77555756156289E-17" w_cz="0"
    w_ixx="8.86144269600547E-05" w_ixy="1.24136366723561E-19" w_ixz="-1.24445298405647E-19"
    w_iyy="5.3339059630236E-05" w_iyz="1.76755868611897E-19" w_izz="5.33390596302369E-05"
    h_col_sx="0.0514" h_col_sy="0.0670" h_col_sz="0.0645"
    h_col_ox="0" h_col_oy="-0.0065" h_col_oz="-0.032"
    w_col_radius="0.03229" w_col_length="0.0350" />

  <!-- back-left -->
  <xacro:caster_corner name="caster_b_l" wheel_name="caster_wheel_b_l"
    housing_mesh="caster_b_l.STL" wheel_mesh="caster_wheel_b_l.STL"
    sx="-0.275000000000001" sy="0.169999999999999" sz="-0.154782629740985"
    h_mass="0.0343773762339354"
    h_cx="-0.000504007629840188" h_cy="-0.00486194519645511" h_cz="-0.0278047373888992"
    h_ixx="7.11069032397272E-06" h_ixy="3.80623065539692E-07" h_ixz="-2.78399345059836E-08"
    h_iyy="1.07445687795745E-05" h_iyz="-2.73284695045809E-07" h_izz="1.35458216922227E-05"
    wx="0.001546" wy="0.01492" wz="-0.05225" wax="1" way="0" waz="0"
    w_mass="0.121190564506901"
    w_cx="0.000258862899684381" w_cy="-2.68228286273153E-05" w_cz="0"
    w_ixx="8.82397102592137E-05" w_ixy="-3.61633193455675E-06" w_ixz="-1.0319517758726E-19"
    w_iyy="5.37137763310779E-05" w_iyz="1.7418729813812E-19" w_izz="5.3339059630236E-05"
    h_col_sx="0.0546" h_col_sy="0.0713" h_col_sz="0.0645"
    h_col_ox="-0.00037" h_col_oy="-0.00659" h_col_oz="-0.032"
    w_col_radius="0.03229" w_col_length="0.0384" />

  <!-- ============================== sensors (new, not in the original CAD/SW export) ==============================
       These mounts are NOT in the SolidWorks assembly - there is no CAD/coordinate-system
       data to pull exact numbers from. Placements were measured directly against the real
       base_link.STL surface: for each mount height, every vertex of the mesh within a thin
       z-band was projected to the XY plane, and the outer radius at each sensor's angle was
       read off that real cross-section (98th-percentile radius in a +/-6 deg window, robust
       to stray facets). Still confirm the visual result in RViz.

       front_sign: this xacro assumes +X = forward. If the camera ends up pointing at the
       robot's back end in RViz, flip this to -1 and re-check. -->
  <xacro:property name="front_sign" value="1.0" />

  <!-- === Intel RealSense D435i, front, fixed ===
       Real D435i housing dimensions (public spec): 90 x 25 x 25 mm. Position measured off
       the real front-face mesh radius at z=-0.06 (~0.3496 m from base_link origin), sitting
       just proud of that surface like a real camera bump. -->
  <xacro:property name="d435i_height_z" value="-0.06" />
  <xacro:property name="d435i_front_x" value="0.345" />

  <link name="d435i_link">
    <visual>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.025 0.090 0.025" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="d435i_black">
        <color rgba="0.05 0.05 0.05 1" />
      </material>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.025 0.090 0.025" />
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.072" /> <!-- real D435i weight, public spec -->
      <inertia ixx="1e-6" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="1e-6" iyz="0.0" izz="1e-6" />
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <joint name="d435i_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link" />
    <child link="d435i_link" />
    <origin xyz="${front_sign * d435i_front_x} 0 ${d435i_height_z}" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </joint>

  <!-- gz-sim camera sensor looks along the LINK's own local +X axis (not the ROS optical-frame
       Z-forward convention) - since d435i_joint has rpy 0 0 0 and the link sits further along
       +X than base_link, no extra rotation is needed here for it to look forward.
       horizontal_fov 1.211259 rad = ~69.4deg, the real D435i RGB stream's public spec.
       Requires the world to load the gz-sim Sensors system (worlds/amr_test_world.sdf) -
       without it this element exists in the SDF but silently never publishes anything. -->
  <gazebo reference="d435i_link">
    <xacro:if value="$(arg enable_cameras)">
      <sensor name="d435i_camera" type="camera">
        <camera>
          <horizontal_fov>1.211259</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>640</width>
            <height>480</height>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.1</near>
            <far>100</far>
          </clip>
        </camera>
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>30</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
        <topic>${robot_name}/camera/d435i</topic>
      </sensor>
    </xacro:if>
    <!-- <visual><material> (not a bare <material> directly under <gazebo reference>) is the
         syntax sdformat's urdf2sdf actually merges into the link's visual - see the GAZEBO
         PHYSICS NOTES header for why the URDF-side <material><color> alone isn't reliable. -->
    <visual>
      <material>
        <ambient>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</ambient>
        <diffuse>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</diffuse>
        <specular>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</specular>
      </material>
    </visual>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- === downward floor/barcode camera, fixed, 8cm above the floor === -->
  <xacro:property name="floor_z" value="-0.244" />
  <xacro:property name="barcode_cam_height_above_floor" value="0.08" />

  <link name="floor_camera_link">
    <visual>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.04 0.04 0.03" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="floor_cam_grey">
        <color rgba="0.2 0.2 0.2 1" />
      </material>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.04 0.04 0.03" />
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.05" />
      <inertia ixx="1e-6" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="1e-6" iyz="0.0" izz="1e-6" />
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <joint name="floor_camera_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link" />
    <child link="floor_camera_link" />
    <!-- pitch = pi/2 about Y so -Z (down in the box's own frame) points straight at the floor -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 ${floor_z + barcode_cam_height_above_floor}" rpy="0 1.5707963 0" />
  </joint>

  <!-- Same gz-sim camera sensor type as the D435i above. Generic placeholder FOV/resolution
       (no real hardware chosen for this one yet, per README) - not a public spec. -->
  <gazebo reference="floor_camera_link">
    <xacro:if value="$(arg enable_cameras)">
      <sensor name="floor_camera" type="camera">
        <camera>
          <horizontal_fov>1.0</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>320</width>
            <height>240</height>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.02</near>
            <far>5</far>
          </clip>
        </camera>
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
        <topic>${robot_name}/camera/floor</topic>
      </sensor>
    </xacro:if>
    <visual>
      <material>
        <ambient>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</ambient>
        <diffuse>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      </material>
    </visual>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- === 12x ultrasonic ring, evenly spaced 30 deg apart, fixed, pointing radially outward ===
       Mounting radius per sensor is the real measured base_link.STL surface radius at that
       angle and height z=-0.05 - the body is a rounded shape, not an ellipse, so radius
       genuinely varies per angle (0.306 m at the sides, up to 0.383 m at the back). A 12mm
       protrusion margin sits each sensor as a visible bump on the surface. -->
  <xacro:property name="ring_height_z" value="-0.05" />
  <xacro:property name="sensor_surface_margin" value="0.012" />

  <xacro:macro name="ultrasonic_sensor" params="index angle_deg radius_m">
    <link name="ultrasonic_${index}_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder radius="0.008" length="0.02" />
        </geometry>
        <material name="ultrasonic_grey">
          <color rgba="0.3 0.3 0.3 1" />
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder radius="0.008" length="0.02" />
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="0.01" />
        <inertia ixx="1e-7" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="1e-7" iyz="0.0" izz="1e-7" />
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="ultrasonic_${index}_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="base_link" />
      <child link="ultrasonic_${index}_link" />
      <origin
        xyz="${(radius_m + sensor_surface_margin) * cos(radians(angle_deg))}
             ${(radius_m + sensor_surface_margin) * sin(radians(angle_deg))}
             ${ring_height_z}"
        rpy="0 1.5707963 ${radians(angle_deg)}" />
    </joint>

    <!-- gz-sim has no dedicated "ultrasonic" sensor type - a single-ray gpu_lidar (1 sample,
         zero-width horizontal/vertical FOV) is the standard simulation stand-in for a sonar
         puck, giving one distance reading per update like a real ultrasonic sensor would.
         range 0.02-4m and resolution are simulation defaults, not a real ultrasonic's public
         spec (no specific hardware chosen yet, matches the rest of this sensor block). -->
    <gazebo reference="ultrasonic_${index}_link">
      <sensor name="ultrasonic_${index}_sensor" type="gpu_lidar">
        <lidar>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>1</samples>
              <min_angle>0</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>1</samples>
              <min_angle>0</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.02</min>
            <max>4.0</max>
            <resolution>0.01</resolution>
          </range>
        </lidar>
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <topic>${robot_name}/ultrasonic_${index}/scan</topic>
      </sensor>
      <visual>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- radius_m = measured real surface radius (m) at that angle, height z=-0.05 -->
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="1"  angle_deg="0"   radius_m="0.3496" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="2"  angle_deg="30"  radius_m="0.3786" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="3"  angle_deg="60"  radius_m="0.3529" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="4"  angle_deg="90"  radius_m="0.3057" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="5"  angle_deg="120" radius_m="0.3530" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="6"  angle_deg="150" radius_m="0.3786" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="7"  angle_deg="180" radius_m="0.3827" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="8"  angle_deg="210" radius_m="0.3786" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="9"  angle_deg="240" radius_m="0.3529" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="10" angle_deg="270" radius_m="0.3059" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="11" angle_deg="300" radius_m="0.3530" />
  <xacro:ultrasonic_sensor index="12" angle_deg="330" radius_m="0.3786" />

  <!-- === 2D planar SLAM lidar, top-front-center, fixed ===
       Not in the original CAD/SW export (same situation as the D435i/floor-camera/
       ultrasonic sensors above) - no CAD coordinate system to pull an exact mount point
       from. Placed top-front-center (x=0.30m forward of base_link origin, z=0.20m up,
       roughly chassis-top height going by the ultrasonic ring's z=-0.05 to -0.15 range
       and floor_z=-0.244), the conventional spot for a 2D lidar puck on an AMR so its
       360 deg sweep isn't blocked by the chassis body. Estimated, not measured - confirm
       clearance against base_link.STL in RViz same as the other added sensors.

       Full 360 deg sweep (min -pi, max pi), not the single-ray ultrasonic style above -
       this is the real SLAM Toolbox / Nav2 costmap input, so it needs a genuine
       horizontal scan. 360 samples = 1 deg resolution, a standard low-cost 2D lidar
       spec (e.g. RPLidar-class); range 0.1-12m and 10Hz likewise standard 2D-lidar
       defaults, not a chosen real part (no hardware picked yet, same caveat as the other
       "no real hardware chosen" sensors in this file). -->
  <!-- x=0.32/z=0.002 (past the footprint edge, flush with the chassis surface) originally
       broke navigation: with the OLD full 360-degree sweep, rays pointed backward/sideways
       from this position hit the robot's own chassis mesh at near-zero range (gz-sim's
       gpu_lidar ray-casts the rendered/visual mesh, not collision geometry, so removing
       lidar_link's <collision> above didn't help) - collision_monitor read that as an
       imminent collision and permanently withheld cmd_vel. Now that the sweep is narrowed
       to 120 degrees centered on forward (+-60deg, see min_angle/max_angle below) and the
       robot only ever drives forward, nothing of the chassis sits inside that forward cone
       from this position (it's past the front edge, chassis is behind/below it), so the
       self-detection problem no longer applies - restored to the requested flush mount. -->
  <xacro:property name="lidar_x" value="0.32" />
  <xacro:property name="lidar_z" value="0.002" />

  <link name="lidar_link">
    <visual>
      <geometry>
        <cylinder radius="0.035" length="0.04" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="lidar_black">
        <color rgba="0.05 0.05 0.05 1" />
      </material>
    </visual>
    <!-- No collision geometry, same reasoning as base_link's own "No collision" note above:
         lidar_link is fixed-jointed to base_link, so dartsim/sdformat LUMPS its collision
         into base_link's rigid body. Whenever lidar_x/lidar_z were tuned close to the
         chassis surface or past its footprint edge (both tried live), that lumped collision
         cylinder started interpenetrating the ground/chassis and froze the robot in place
         (confirmed live: /odom position stopped changing entirely, cmd_vel chain otherwise
         fine) - a small sensor puck doesn't need its own physics collision anyway. -->
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.08" />
      <inertia ixx="1e-6" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="1e-6" iyz="0.0" izz="1e-6" />
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <joint name="lidar_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link" />
    <child link="lidar_link" />
    <origin xyz="${front_sign * lidar_x} 0 ${lidar_z}" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </joint>

  <!-- Narrowed to a 120-degree forward-facing sweep (+-60deg) per explicit request, down
       from the full 360-degree sweep. This intentionally gives up rear/side coverage - the
       robot's own chassis is no longer in view (see the self-detection/collision_monitor
       lockup this file's history notes elsewhere), but so is any real obstacle behind or
       beside the robot; SLAM Toolbox and Nav2's costmaps will only ever see what's ahead.
       samples reduced from 360 to 120 to keep the same ~1deg/sample resolution over the
       smaller range, not a resolution change. topic prefixed with robot_name (see the
       multi-robot property comment near the top of this file) since gz-transport topics
       are global, not per-model. -->
  <gazebo reference="lidar_link">
    <sensor name="lidar_sensor" type="gpu_lidar">
      <lidar>
        <scan>
          <horizontal>
            <samples>120</samples>
            <min_angle>-1.0471975512</min_angle>
            <max_angle>1.0471975512</max_angle>
          </horizontal>
          <vertical>
            <samples>1</samples>
            <min_angle>0</min_angle>
            <max_angle>0</max_angle>
          </vertical>
        </scan>
        <range>
          <min>0.1</min>
          <max>12.0</max>
          <resolution>0.01</resolution>
        </range>
      </lidar>
      <always_on>true</always_on>
      <update_rate>10</update_rate>
      <visualize>true</visualize>
      <topic>${robot_name}/scan</topic>
      <!-- Without this, gz-sim's LaserScan bridge stamped every message with frame_id
           "<model>/base_link/lidar_sensor" instead of "lidar_link" - sdformat's URDF->SDF
           conversion apparently lumps this fixed-jointed child link into base_link for
           frame-naming purposes despite lidar_link having its own mass/inertia (confirmed
           live in WSL: slam_toolbox logged "Message Filter dropping message: frame
           'robot1/base_link/lidar_sensor' ... discarding message because the queue is
           full" forever - no TF exists for that composite name, so scans were silently
           discarded and no map was ever built). <gz_frame_id> is gz-sensors' explicit
           override for exactly this situation - forces the published frame_id to match
           the plain "lidar_link" name robot_state_publisher actually publishes a
           base_link->lidar_link transform for. -->
      <gz_frame_id>lidar_link</gz_frame_id>
    </sensor>
    <!-- <visual><material> (not a bare <material> directly under <gazebo reference>) - see
         the "Gazebo render materials" comment block below for why the other syntax
         silently gets dropped by sdformat's urdf2sdf. -->
    <visual>
      <material>
        <ambient>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</ambient>
        <diffuse>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</diffuse>
        <specular>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</specular>
      </material>
    </visual>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- ============================== Gazebo render materials ==============================
       gz sim's URDF->SDF conversion does not reliably carry the <material><color> tags
       above into the SDF visuals, so without this the whole robot renders flat default-grey
       in Gazebo even though RViz shows the real colors. The mechanism that actually works is
       <gazebo reference="link"><visual><material>... (NOT a bare <material> directly under
       <gazebo reference>, which sdformat's urdf2sdf does not merge into the visual - that
       was the mistake in an earlier version of this file, and why the robot was still all
       grey after adding it). The two camera links (d435i_link, floor_camera_link) get their
       <visual><material> alongside their <sensor> block instead of a separate <gazebo
       reference> here, since a link can only sensibly own one merged gazebo extension. -->
  <xacro:macro name="body_material" params="link color">
    <gazebo reference="${link}">
      <visual>
        <material>
          <ambient>${color}</ambient>
          <diffuse>${color}</diffuse>
          <specular>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>
  <xacro:body_material link="base_link" color="${chassis_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="left_wheel" color="${wheel_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="right_wheel" color="${wheel_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_f_l" color="${caster_housing_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_wheel_f_l" color="${wheel_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_f_r" color="${caster_housing_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_wheel_f_r" color="${wheel_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_b_r" color="${caster_housing_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_wheel_b_r" color="${wheel_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_b_l" color="${caster_housing_color}" />
  <xacro:body_material link="caster_wheel_b_l" color="${wheel_color}" />

  <!-- ============================== drive wheel-ground friction ==============================
       mu1/mu2=1.0 for real traction - without any friction set, wheels spin at the correct
       commanded velocity but the chassis never translates (zero-friction wheel-slip). Not a
       measured tire property (no real hardware exists yet), just a standard rubber default. -->
  <xacro:macro name="wheel_friction" params="link">
    <gazebo reference="${link}">
      <mu1>1.0</mu1>
      <mu2>1.0</mu2>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>
  <xacro:wheel_friction link="left_wheel" />
  <xacro:wheel_friction link="right_wheel" />

  <!-- ============================== caster wheel-ground friction ==============================
       LOW (0.15), not 1.0 like the drive wheels: casters only support weight, they don't
       need traction, and their self-aligning trail is small (~15mm vs ~37.6mm wheel radius)
       so a skewed caster under high friction scrubs instead of rolling - enough drag to
       overpower the drive wheels entirely. Not near-zero either (0.05 let the chassis creep
       after a stop instead of settling) - 0.15 is the middle ground. -->
  <xacro:macro name="caster_wheel_friction" params="link">
    <gazebo reference="${link}">
      <mu1>0.15</mu1>
      <mu2>0.15</mu2>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>
  <xacro:caster_wheel_friction link="caster_wheel_f_l" />
  <xacro:caster_wheel_friction link="caster_wheel_f_r" />
  <xacro:caster_wheel_friction link="caster_wheel_b_r" />
  <xacro:caster_wheel_friction link="caster_wheel_b_l" />

  <!-- ============================== ros2_control (Gazebo phase) ==============================
       Only the two drive wheels are actuated. Casters stay passive (no <joint> entry here) -
       Gazebo just lets them spin/swivel freely under contact + the dynamics on their <joint>
       tags above.

       Command/state interface = velocity only, so no PID gains need to be invented -
       gz_ros2_control drives the velocity command interface directly as a joint velocity
       target. effort/velocity bounds match the wheel joints (SolidWorks CSV: effort=20 N-m,
       velocity=30 rad/s). -->
  <ros2_control name="amr_ros_dg_system" type="system">
    <hardware>
      <plugin>gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem</plugin>
    </hardware>
    <!-- Raised from 30.0 -> 60.0 rad/s: at wheel_radius=0.075, 30 rad/s caps linear speed at
         2.25 m/s, which was silently clamping Nav2's 2.0 m/s cruise target (near the ceiling,
         no headroom for turns) - this was the actual reason the robot still looked slow after
         nav2_params.yaml/amr_ros_dg_controllers.yaml were raised. 60 rad/s allows 4.5 m/s. -->
    <joint name="left_wheel_joint">
      <command_interface name="velocity">
        <param name="min">-60.0</param>
        <param name="max">60.0</param>
      </command_interface>
      <state_interface name="velocity" />
      <state_interface name="position" />
    </joint>
    <joint name="right_wheel_joint">
      <command_interface name="velocity">
        <param name="min">-60.0</param>
        <param name="max">60.0</param>
      </command_interface>
      <state_interface name="velocity" />
      <state_interface name="position" />
    </joint>
  </ros2_control>

  <gazebo>
    <plugin filename="gz_ros2_control-system" name="gz_ros2_control::GazeboSimROS2ControlPlugin">
      <parameters>$(find amr_ros_dg)/config/amr_ros_dg_controllers.yaml</parameters>
      <!-- Fleet mode only (see namespace_controller_manager property comment above): without
           this <ros><namespace> tag, gz_ros2_control's controller_manager node comes up
           unnamespaced regardless of robot_name, and subscribes to a plain "robot_description"
           topic that no robot in a namespaced fleet ever publishes (each publishes
           /robotN/robot_description) - it just hangs forever logging "Waiting for data on
           robot_description topic to finish initialization", which is exactly what happened
           before this was added (confirmed live in WSL: 3 robots spawned fine, but zero
           /robotN/joint_states ever appeared, and every controller/diff_drive spawner timed
           out with "Failed to acquire lock" waiting on a controller_manager that never came up). -->
      <!-- Using $(arg ...) directly (not the namespace_controller_manager PROPERTY) as the
           xacro:if condition - xacro has built-in true/false string recognition for raw
           $(arg) substitutions used this way; going through the property indirection
           (${namespace_controller_manager == 'true'}) silently evaluated false always (a
           real bug hit and fixed here: `xacro robot_name:=robot1
           namespace_controller_manager:=true` produced NO <ros><namespace> tag in the
           output at all, confirmed with `xacro ... | grep -A6 GazeboSimROS2ControlPlugin`). -->
      <xacro:if value="$(arg namespace_controller_manager)">
        <ros>
          <namespace>${robot_name}</namespace>
          <!-- diff_drive_controller's tf2_ros::TransformBroadcaster publishes to the
               HARDCODED absolute topic "/tf" (a long-standing tf2 behavior - it does not
               respect node namespacing like ordinary topics do), so without this remap
               every robot's odom->base_link transform lands on the single global /tf
               topic instead of /robotN/tf. Symptom seen live in WSL: Nav2's local_costmap
               logged "Timed out waiting for transform from base_link to odom... frame does
               not exist" forever (it only looks on /robotN/tf) even though
               diff_drive_controller was confirmed active and definitely computing odometry.
               nav2_bringup's own navigation_launch.py applies this exact same remap to all
               ITS nodes for the same reason - controller_manager (spawned by this Gazebo
               plugin, not by a launch Node()) needed it added separately here since launch
               remappings=[] can't reach a node Gazebo spawns internally.
               Remap TARGET is the fully-qualified absolute topic (/${robot_name}/tf), not
               a bare relative "tf" - confirmed live in WSL that the relative form does NOT
               work here either (same fix applied to fleet.launch.py's robot_state_publisher
               and slam_toolbox Node()s, see their comments for the full story). -->
          <remapping>/tf:=/${robot_name}/tf</remapping>
          <remapping>/tf_static:=/${robot_name}/tf_static</remapping>
        </ros>
      </xacro:if>
    </plugin>
  </gazebo>

</robot>
